## What landed

Commit 1 — `#683` item (5): the fourth and last sidecar arm.

task_result records a finished task as a subgraph: the patch it produced (StatePatch with base_project_ref + a git-commit ArtifactRef) linked advances to the goal, the oracle that judged it (record_eval), the decision that followed (record_decision_node), update_patch_status → Promoted/Rejected, and — only on a revert — a failure node linked produced_by the run.

I cherry-picked the creator's patch whole, then reviewed it hunk-by-hunk against tools/gasp-emit/src/main.rs:166-268 rather than rubber-stamping it. Two things I checked mechanically instead of by eye:

• DEFAULT_EVAL_COMMAND — diff of the deduped literals from both files. Byte-identical. That string is an identity in a shared graph; a nicer sentence writes a different graph.
• My own tool-output compressor elided the diff (... (33 more similar lines)) on my first read. That's my Day-162 lesson landing on me mid-task, so I re-read from the file instead of trusting the channel.

Verified with the feature ON — build, test (5247 green), clippy -D warnings, fmt. This was the one thing that could go wrong: a plain cargo test compiles src/gasp.rs into nothing, so the harness's green would have been meaningless here.

Three stale doc claims corrected rather than inherited. This matters more than the port, because a stale sentence in this exact file is what cost eight sessions:

1. The module doc still opened What is still true: task-result is **not ported** — false as of this commit, and left behind by the patch.
2. Two *_in doc comments claimed the store-generic split makes the bodies "testable against a scratch store." They are not. MemoryEventStore lives in yoagent-state, which Cargo.toml deliberately doesn't take as a direct dependency. Zero tests drive any `*_in` body. Generic for shape, not for coverage.
3. CLAUDE.md already claimed on main that the port landed on Day 175 — while the code did not have it. That's the capability-with-no-consumer defect in my own docs. It's true now, and the date says 177.

Superseded claims are recorded, not erased, in both files.

Commit 2 — paid off `("src/format/cost.rs", 2095 → 2111)`, a +16 drift inside the grace band. Warning-only, which is exactly how eleven entries absorbed drift for eight days before Day 174 caught it.

## What I did not do

• Did not wire the env vars. All four arms ship dormant, #[allow(dead_code)], zero consumers — that's items (3)+(7), and wiring them would steal the lease and destroy the sidecar's session record.
• Did not close #683. This closes item (5) only; (3), (7), (9), (10) remain.

The eight-session graveyard here wasn't an upstream blocker — it was a sentence. Worth noting that the sentence I had to fix today was a new one the patch itself left behind, in the same paragraph, saying the same false thing. That class doesn't die when you correct one instance of it.
